\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.1}{\ignorespaces QLIKE vs.\ MSE penalty as a function of the forecast ratio $h_t / \sigma ^2_t$. Both losses are minimized at the perfect forecast ($h_t = \sigma ^2_t$, ratio $= 1$). MSE penalizes over- and under-prediction symmetrically. QLIKE penalizes under-prediction (ratio $< 1$) much more harshly than over-prediction (ratio $> 1$), matching the asymmetric risk preferences in volatility forecasting: underestimating vol means holding too much risk. Despite this asymmetry, both losses are minimized at ratio $= 1$ (the true variance); the asymmetry shapes the penalty curve, not the optimal forecast.}}{319}{figure.18.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qlike-vs-mse}{{18.1}{319}{QLIKE vs.\ MSE penalty as a function of the forecast ratio $h_t / \sigma ^2_t$. Both losses are minimized at the perfect forecast ($h_t = \sigma ^2_t$, ratio $= 1$). MSE penalizes over- and under-prediction symmetrically. QLIKE penalizes under-prediction (ratio $< 1$) much more harshly than over-prediction (ratio $> 1$), matching the asymmetric risk preferences in volatility forecasting: underestimating vol means holding too much risk. Despite this asymmetry, both losses are minimized at ratio $= 1$ (the true variance); the asymmetry shapes the penalty curve, not the optimal forecast}{figure.18.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:qlike-vs-mse@cref}{{[figure][1][18]18.1}{[1][318][]319}{}{}{}}
