\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20.2}{\ignorespaces The dollar-gamma weight $S^2\Gamma $ as a function of the underlying price, for a fixed strike $K$. The weight that converts a variance gap into P\&L is concentrated near the money and decays as the stock drifts into either wing. This is why a single delta-hedged straddle is a \emph  {path-dependent} variance bet: the same realized-minus-implied gap earns more money on days the stock loiters near the strike than on days it trends away.}}{364}{figure.20.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ivrv:dollar-gamma}{{20.2}{364}{The dollar-gamma weight $S^2\Gamma $ as a function of the underlying price, for a fixed strike $K$. The weight that converts a variance gap into P\&L is concentrated near the money and decays as the stock drifts into either wing. This is why a single delta-hedged straddle is a \emph {path-dependent} variance bet: the same realized-minus-implied gap earns more money on days the stock loiters near the strike than on days it trends away}{figure.20.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:ivrv:dollar-gamma@cref}{{[figure][2][20]20.2}{[1][364][]364}{}{}{}}
