\newlabel{sec:gsvivs:classification}{{21.10}{388}{Drawdown Prediction as Cost-Sensitive Classification}{section.21.10}{}}
\newlabel{sec:gsvivs:classification@cref}{{[section][10][21]21.10}{[1][388][]388}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21.6}{\ignorespaces The cost-sensitive structure of drawdown classification. The four outcomes carry wildly different payoffs: a false negative (predicting safety into a crash) costs a full drawdown, while a false positive (a needless flat day) costs only one day's forgone premium. With drawdowns at roughly $12$--$15\%$ of days and this cost ratio, the decision threshold must be tuned to minimize expected cost, not classification error.}}{389}{figure.21.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gsvivs:costmatrix}{{21.6}{389}{The cost-sensitive structure of drawdown classification. The four outcomes carry wildly different payoffs: a false negative (predicting safety into a crash) costs a full drawdown, while a false positive (a needless flat day) costs only one day's forgone premium. With drawdowns at roughly $12$--$15\%$ of days and this cost ratio, the decision threshold must be tuned to minimize expected cost, not classification error}{figure.21.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:gsvivs:costmatrix@cref}{{[figure][6][21]21.6}{[1][389][]389}{}{}{}}
